WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.420
Suppose you specify two tables in a query without&nbsp;
specifying a join. In that case, you will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:12.480
a cartesian product in which every record in one&nbsp;
table is related to every record in another. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.840
is usually done by accident, but, as we see with&nbsp;
this query, we are trying to display an inventory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:26.160
sheet of every product in every location. Notice,&nbsp;
that with these two tables, Product and Location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.460
no specific join is specified, which indicates&nbsp;
a cartesian product. When I run this query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.940
every product is in every&nbsp;
location and one can print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.780
this out and start taking inventory of&nbsp;
the products that are in each location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.020
If we want to, for the purpose of clarifying&nbsp;
the use of a query such as this query, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:55.560
indicate that this query is a CROSS JOIN, which&nbsp;
is a cartesian product. I will change the comma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:02.220
to the words CROSS JOIN and execute this query&nbsp;
again. The results are the same, so whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.860
one uses a comma or a CROSS JOIN clause, the&nbsp;
end result will still be a Cartesian product.

